Lets compute the cross product of the standard basis vectors for R³. 
First consider i x j as shown in figure 1.53. 
The vectors i and j determine a sqare of unit area. 
Thus ||i x j || must be perpendicular to the plane in whih i and j lie. 
Hence, i x j must pointin the directions of +-k. 
The "right-hand rule" implies that i x j must point in the positive k direction
Since ||k|| = 1, we conclude that i x j = k. 
The same argument establishes that j x k = i and k x i = j. 
To remember these basic equations, you can draw i, j, and k in a circle, as a figure 1.54. 
Then the relations

		i x j = k,  	j x k = i, 	k x i = j

may be read from the circle by beginning at any vector and then proceeding clockwise. 
